#{[17592186512693 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512702 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512707 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512710 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512712 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512718 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186512864 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$"] [17592186512864 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512872 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186512872 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512878 :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186512878 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1} &=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-14{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -14 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [17592186512886 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186512886 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ 3+{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512887 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 & 15 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186512887 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}39 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186512893 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186512893 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512909 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186512909 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512915 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 13 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186512915 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512927 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$"] [17592186512927 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512933 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186512933 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186512934 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & -5 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$"] [17592186512934 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512943 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186512943 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512945 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t}$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186512945 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}(-s+t+1){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512949 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186512954 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$"] [17592186512954 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512957 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s-1},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186512957 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3{e}^{1\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\}-2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot t}-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512958 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 23 & 16 \\\\ 14 & 20 & 20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186512958 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}54 \\\\ 54\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \