\\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186513979 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186513987 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186513992 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514011 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514019 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514025 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514048 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514073 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514077 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514082 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514084 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514090 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514110 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514120 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514148 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514175 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186514363 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$"] [17592186514363 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186514369 :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1})$, if $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186514369 :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186514375 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $4,$ and $8.$"] [17592186514375 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]}
